e following:\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle {\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{1} & \\displaystyle  = {a}_{11} = 1 > 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{2} & \\displaystyle  = \\left| \\begin{array}{cc}\\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 1 \\\\ \\displaystyle 1 & \\displaystyle 2\\end{array} \\right| = 1 > 0 \\\\ \\displaystyle {\\mathrm{\\Delta }}_{3} & \\displaystyle  = | A | = - 2 < 0\\end{array}\n$$\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite.\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the matrix $A$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, the critical point $\\mathbf a$ is a saddle point of $f.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186124379 1074 "The point $\\mathbf{a} \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a critical point of the function $f: \\Bbb R^n \\to \\Bbb R$ if $\\mathbf{f}'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$ In such cases, we can classify $\\mathbf a$ as follows:\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is positive definite, then $\\mathbf a$ is a local minimum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is negative definite, then $\\mathbf a$ is a local maximum\n\n- if $\\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a})$ is neither positive-definite nor negative-definite, then $\\mathbf a$ is a saddle point\n\nIn this case, we have $A = \\mathbf{f}''(\\mathbf{a}).$ Now, before we examine the statements, we recall how to categorize quadratic forms.\n\nLet $\\Delta_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if $\\Delta_i > 0$ for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,\\ldots,n,$ we have $\\Delta_i < 0$ when $i$ is odd, and $\\Delta_i > 0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed