zed by varying $v$ only. So, the position vectors of $A$ and $B,$ respectively, are given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf a\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} + \\delta  u , {v}_{0} )\\end{array} \\right]}, \n\n\\qquad \n\n\\mathbf b\n= \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}x \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v ) \\\\ y \\hspace{-0.167em} ( {u}_{0} , {v}_{0} + \\delta  v )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since $\\delta u$ is small, $S_1'$ can be approximated by a line segment. Similarly, since $\\delta v$ is small, $S_2'$ can also be approximated by a line segment. Let's construct two vectors $\\mathbf{s}_1$ and $\\mathbf{s}_2$ that span our two segments, as shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1ff10cdcde237698a28c6f76053de50745ef12c67dfc5290ac6e06893d201972.png)\n\nWe can find the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ by subtracting the position vectors of its endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{1}=a-\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{0} \\\\ {y}_{0}\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0}) \\\\ y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that when $\\delta u$ is small, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{∂x}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{x\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-x\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u} \\\\ {\\frac{∂y}{∂u}|}_{({u}_{0},{v}_{0})} & ≈\\frac{y\\,({u}_{0}+\\delta u,{v}_{0})-y\\,({u}_{0},{v}_{0})}{\\delta u}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vector $\\mathbf s_1$ can be approximated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf s_1 \\approx\n\\delta u \\cdot {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}{\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  x}{\\partial  u}} \\\\ {\\displaystyle \\frac{\\partial  y}{\\partial  u}}\\end{array} \\right]}_{(u_0,v_0)}\