\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Number of Courses</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$4$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\"><strong>Average Grade</strong></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$80$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$85$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$75$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$72$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m,$ is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 80\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 85\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}5 \\\\ 75\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_4 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 72\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  &