ence between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 80\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 85\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 75\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={x}_{4}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 72\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 78\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\nB =\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}0 & - 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & - 3 & - 6\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X.$ It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{B} & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186163321 1064 "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n$$\nX=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cccc}4 & 3 & 5 & 4 \\\\ 80 & 85 & 75 & 72\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}_1 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}4 \\\\ 80\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_2 = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 85\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\\mathbf{x}_3 = {\\left[ \\begin{ar