al normal vector $N(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nN(t)=\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the principal normal $N(t).$ To do this, we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.32$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186168006 1064 "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is $\\mathbf{v} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}.$ The vector $\\mathbf{w} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}$ is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n$$\n\\lambda = 1, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{v} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad\n\\mathbf{w} =  {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}0 \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$ \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}t{e}^{t}+\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}{e}^{t}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}t \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n$$\nc_1 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n$$\nc_2 e^{t} {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}t \\\\ 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$t$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.5$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$x$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$-0.30$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.98$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.61$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3.32$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186171711 1074 "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the surface integral of a scalar function $f$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S f(x,y,z) \\, \\textrm{d}S \n=\n\\iint\\limits_D f(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\right\\|  \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nIn vector form, our surface has the equation \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\left\\langle u+v, \\: u-v, \\: v\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}1,\\hspace{0.222em}0⟩ \\\\ {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u+v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u-v),\\hspace{0.222em}\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\hspace{0.222em}-1,\\hspace{0.222em}1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}_{u}^{'}\\times {\\mathbf{r}}_{v}^{'} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\