592186576411 false] [17592186429765 1080 "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186429765 1080 "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186429766 1080 "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186429766 1080 "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186429767 1080 "B=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186429767 1080 "B=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\}" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186429769 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186429769 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{2}},\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186429960 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d710469b744813cd9f54ea3234f0c7343d35.png)\n\n \n\nNow, we consider some general solutions of the form\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=2t+3 \\\\ y &=t+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/40/4019f96cd3c03ec9b0349a0a896951bfa4b0b8f0f828442e002de28d7c8ceb92.png)\n\n- When ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=-1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=-2t-3 \\\\ y &=-t-1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nLet's add it to the phase space:\n\n![](images/df/df4f8378d0c02f982299a349e76c204fd60fb5e014b72e01545cc5dec358aa04.png)\n\n \n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/18/1871e29694b88593a1aeb9ac87536059361f30a2e39966a9ca1180067213d15c.png)" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186429960 1074 "Since we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =0$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $v,$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}v{e}^{\\lambda t}+{c}_{2}(vt{e}^{\\lambda t}+w{e}^{\\lambda t}\\,),\n$$\n\nwhere $w$ is a solution to the equation $(A-\\lambda I)w=v.$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\lambda =0,\\,v=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThus, the general solution of the system is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix} & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}t+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\right) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+{c}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2t+1 \\\\ t\\end{bmatrix},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that ${x}_{E}(t)={c}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ are equilibrium solutions of the system since ${x}_{E}^{′}(t)=0.$ These solutions form the line with the equation $y=\\frac{1}{2}x$ in the phase space, as shown below.\n\n![](images/5a/5afce1bca483dbd9bd462810c351d