$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{2}$ and ${v}_{3}.$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186455477 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=z.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx=2y-z=2z-z=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\right\\}.$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186455477 1074 "Finding the kernel of $T$ is equivalent to finding the null space of the standard matrix of $T.$\n\nFror the given linear transformation, the standard matrix is $T=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nSetting $x=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix},$ we then need to solve the equation $Tx=0.$ First, we find reduce $T$ to row echelon form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-6{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9 & -9 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{9}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variable is $x$ and $y$, and we have one free variable, $z.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $y$ in terms of $z.$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get $y=z.$ Substituting these into the first equation, we have \n\n$$\nx=2y-z=2z-z=z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the kernel of the linear transformation $\\,T$ is $\\{\\begin{bmatrix}z \\\\ z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\,:\\,z\\in R\\}.$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186455506 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix})=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{3})=0,$ we conclude that the only vectors that belong to the kernel of $\\,T$ are $\\,{v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{3}.$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186455506 1074 "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T\\left(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\right)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSin