592186576411 false] [17592186522781 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\right.\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$ \n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $A$ is not invertible" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186522781 1071 "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix and $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}?\n$$ \n\nI. $\\text{rank}(A)=3$\n\nII. $\\text{Col}(A)={R}^{4}$\n\nIII. $A$ is not invertible" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186522781 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\right.\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}=\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4,$ then, by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186522781 1074 "Since $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $$\n\\displaystyle \\,⇔\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{4} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=4=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, if $A$ is a matrix with $n$ columns, then \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since \n\n$$\n\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1} \\\\ -3{x}_{1} \\\\ -4{x}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,{x}_{1}\\in R\\}=\\text{Span}\\{\\,\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\}\n$$\n\n \nwe have that $\\text{nullity}(A)=1.$\n\nNow,  for the matrix $A,$ the number of columns is $n=4,$ so according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(A)+\\text{nullity}(A) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(A)+1 & =4 \\\\ \\text{rank}(A) & =4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4,$ then, by the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\text{Col}(A)\\ne {R}^{4}.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $\\text{rank}(A)=3\\ne 4$, according to the  invertible matrix theorem $A$ can't be an invertible matrix.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186529336 1074 "For a surface with parametrization $r(u,v),$ the fundamental vector product is defined as ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}.$\n\nIn vector form, our surface ha