ar combination of vectors ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=1\\cdot {a}_{1}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{2}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186596666 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\left\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\right\\}.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186596666 1074 "Remember that a basis of $S$ is a set of linearly independent vectors that spans $S.$\n\nFrom the given vectors, we can see that ${a}_{1}$ is a linear combination of vectors ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}:$\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=1\\cdot {a}_{2}+1\\cdot {a}_{3}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the vector ${a}_{1}$ can be removed from $\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ without changing the span. So $S=\\text{Span}\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is spanned by just $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}.$ \n\nLastly, note that ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{3}$ are linearly independent since they are not parallel. So the set $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\}$ is a linearly independent set that spans $S.$\n\nTherefore, a basis of $S$ is $\\{{a}_{2},{a}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{{{\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}},{{\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}\\}.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186597539 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186597539 1071 "$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186597539 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}(\\text{Span}\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\})=3.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 false] [17592186597539 1074 "We start by creating a matrix whose columns are made up of our vectors, and then we reduce the matrix to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 8 & 12\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{4} & :={R}_{4}+(-4){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to basis vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the reduced matrix has $3$ pivot columns (or alternatively, it has exactly $3$ non-zero rows). \n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{dim}\\left(\\text{Span}\\left\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix}\\right\\}\\right)=3.\n$$" 13194139544863 17592186576411 true] [17592186597544 1071 "Determine the dimension of\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text